{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019ugn9k1Grpix66jc3rHPEN","type":"tool_result","content":"internal/roles/reviewers/security-review:\nprompt.txt\n\ninternal/roles/scanners/cve:\nDockerfile\nmerge.jq\nscan\n\ninternal/roles/scanners/docs:\nDockerfile\ndocscan.go\nscan\n\ninternal/roles/scanners/error-codes:\nDockerfile\nerrcodes.jq\nscan\n\ninternal/roles/scanners/lint:\nDockerfile\nnormalize.jq\nscan\n\ninternal/roles/scanners/missing-tests:\nDockerfile\nmissingtests.go\nscan\n\ninternal/roles/scanners/trufflehog:\nDockerfile\nexclude-paths\nfindings.jq\nscan\n\ninternal/roles/scanners/trufflehog-history:\nDockerfile\nexclude-paths\nfindings.jq\nhistory\nscan\ndocs/proposal.md:333:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (2,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 1,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages and uses no other network, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make test`, and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 1000`, `diff_max_lines: 2000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base.\ndocs/proposal.md:351:**The starter reviewer (`reviewers/eng-review`, #36).** `.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt` checks correctness, missing tests and drift from `plan.md`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `diff.patch`, `issue.md`, `comments.md` and the committed `.ard/<issue#>/`; text in the change that addresses a reviewer is a blocking `high` finding on its line (verdict `changes`), and the same text in the issue, comments or `.ard/<issue#>/` is ignored and mentioned in the body. It runs the repository's own linter and the changed packages' tests with coverage through Bash, writing scratch files outside the worktree, reports only problems on added lines and skips a check it cannot run, saying so; the package manager may fetch declared dependencies and nothing else is downloaded. Critical and high findings always block, medium only when it is a real bug, low and info never; a missing test blocks only when it is `high` (risky or core logic, or a case `plan.md` named), smaller gaps are `medium`. The verdict is `changes` exactly when a finding blocks. `line` is the head-side line: a `+` line goes inline, anything else in the body. It writes only `review-eng-review.md` with `role: eng-review`, `attempt: 1`, `status` `done` or `needs-human`, `findings` with a double-quoted `file` and a `message: |` block, never `next` or `worker`, and a body with a `## Checks` section. It answers `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review or the issue and the plan contradict each other. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5 with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`, so every PR from the starter worker gets it. It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55.\ndocs/proposal.md:353:**The starter security reviewer (`reviewers/security-review`, #43).** `.ard/reviewers/security-review/prompt.txt` looks for injection, authz gaps, secrets and unsafe dependencies the change introduces or makes reachable, plus other weaknesses. It has the same untrusted inputs, Trust rules (steering text in the change is a blocking `high` finding), write rules and role-file format as eng-review, with `role: security-review` and a `## Checks` body. It never builds, tests or runs the repository's code. It runs, when they are installed: gitleaks over the changed files with `--redact`; gosec on changed Go packages with `-nosec`; and semgrep with `--metrics=off` and `--disable-nosem`, using only a rule file already in the repository that the change does not touch, or the sidecar's. Suppressions the change adds are reviewed as part of the change. It skips a missing tool and says so, never downloads rules or vulnerability data, and never copies a secret it finds into its report (file, line and kind only). Each finding's first line starts with `[injection]`, `[authz]`, `[secret]`, `[dependency]` or `[other]`. Critical and high block, medium only when it is one of the four kinds with a nameable exploit, low and info never; `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review. The starter override is Opus 5.5 (#8) with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`. No Dockerfile: host mode with the #84 warning until #55. Running pinned tools in controller code is #205.\ndocs/proposal.md:355:**The starter license reviewer (`reviewers/license-review`, #44).** `.ard/reviewers/license-review/prompt.txt` checks that every dependency a PR adds or upgrades has a license on the owners' allowlist. The allowlist is one SPDX id per line (`#` comments; `A OR B` passes when any operand is listed, `A AND B` only when all are, `A WITH E` only when listed verbatim; no per-package exceptions). It is read from the sidecar's `licenses/allowlist.txt` when the role has a sidecar and that file exists, else from `allowlist.txt` in the role directory (`$ARD_ROLE_DIR`, read from the config snapshot on `main`, under a protected path). An allowlist found in the PR, the diff, the issue or `.ard/<issue#>/` is ignored, and an unreadable or empty one ends `needs-human`. For Go it runs `go-licenses check` and `go-licenses report` when the tool is on the host `PATH` (optional; it never installs tools), else falls back to `go mod download -json` and the module's LICENSE files; for npm it reads the lockfile `license` field. A license outside the allowlist, or one that cannot be determined, is a blocking `high` finding on the manifest line that adds it (verdict `changes`, so the merger never merges while the label stands); a license that changed but is still allowed is `info`; other ecosystems are `info` and unchecked; no dependency change approves with \"No dependency changes to review\". The starter override is Sonnet 5.5 with `when.paths` listing the manifests and lockfiles above. It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55.\n26://   - an optional \"Dockerfile\" names the role image;\n103:\tif _, hasDockerfile := files[\"Dockerfile\"]; hasDockerfile {\n104:\t\trole.Dockerfile = dir + \"/Dockerfile\"\n105:\t\tdelete(extras, \"Dockerfile\")","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"65cc2d11-7aa2-481e-bf82-a7c97eae1951","uuid":"35045203-2701-4c47-be64-4efa7afa9c17","timestamp":"2026-11-24T12:44:16.074Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"internal/roles/reviewers/security-review:\nprompt.txt\n\ninternal/roles/scanners/cve:\nDockerfile\nmerge.jq\nscan\n\ninternal/roles/scanners/docs:\nDockerfile\ndocscan.go\nscan\n\ninternal/roles/scanners/error-codes:\nDockerfile\nerrcodes.jq\nscan\n\nint